d TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n13|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n14|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n15|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n16|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n17|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n18|\n19|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, and traceability.go pointing at that trio. Cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above. If origin/main moves again before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate <this task>` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base.\n20|\n21|YOUR PREVIOUS ATTEMPT FAILED AT CR CONSTRUCTION FOR A PROVENANCE REASON, NOT FOR ANYTHING IN YOUR WORK — READ THIS FIRST. The construction refused with:\n22|`delivery failure [stale-anchor]: change_request_base_authority_mismatch: the STORY-260830-1cyj0q candidate provenance disagrees: checkpoint 47294bb8 does not descend from selected authority c3aae73 while branch=47294bb8 and head=47294bb8`.\n23|Nothing about that is your content. The Story branch was cut before trunk advanced: its tip is the TASK-260830-2g5be6 checkpoint sitting on base a12d1bd, while current origin/main is c3aae73 (PR #54 landed STORY-260830-4qojoz, which also raised the configured suite from 27 to 30 commands). A Change Request must descend from the selected authority, so the branch has to move onto c3aae73 before any CR can be constructed here.\n24|So, BEFORE you do anything else: run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-32ypr2`. You are the live tracked producer for this initial leaf and it provably has no Change Request, which is exactly the case that command exists for; it preserves your complete working candidate exactly and replays the two checkpoints (TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev6 and TASK-260830-2g5be6 rev3) with signing in an isolated workspace. If a regular-file conflict appears, prepare `--replay-resolutions` from the retained replay worktree's `resolution-template.json`, binding each resolution to REBASE_HEAD, its unmerged path and the SHA-256 of the explicit replacement content — keep LOGBOOK entries newest-first and never resolve by discarding trunk content.\n25|After the refresh, audit every path in `git diff --name-only a12d1bd c3aae73` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md, LOGBOOK.md and task-board.config.json all moved on trunk, so your edits must sit on top of the NEW content, never restore the old bytes, and the config must end byte-identical to the new base. Then read the command count from the candidate config rather than assuming it — it is 30 now, not 27.\n26|Everything you already wrote in the worktree is preserved and still stands: start with `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, state in your results what you inherited, and verify it against this brief instead of redoing it. Your scope is unchanged.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
