chor, anchor+1, index+1)\n111|\t\t\t}\n112|\t\t\tanchor = index\n113|\t\t}\n114|\t}\n115|\tif anchor < 0 {\n116|\t\tt.Fatalf(\"README no longer carries %q\", measuredCoverageAnchor)\n117|\t}\n118|\tend := -1\n119|\tfor index := anchor + 1; index < len(lines); index++ {\n120|\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(lines[index])\n121|\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"## \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"### \") {\n122|\t\t\tend = index\n123|\t\t\tbreak\n124|\t\t}\n125|\t}\n126|\tif end < 0 {\n127|\t\tt.Fatalf(\"README subsection %q runs to end of file without a closing heading\", measuredCoverageAnchor)\n128|\t}\n129|\treturn lines[anchor+1 : end], anchor + 2\n130|}\n131|\n132|// assertFencedCoverageLine requires the subsection to carry exactly one\n133|// fenced block and its content to equal the tool's coverage line\n134|// byte-for-byte: no rewording, no reordered fields, no rounding.\n135|func assertFencedCoverageLine(t *testing.T, subsection []string, start int, want string) {\n136|\tt.Helper()\n137|\n138|\tvar fences []int\n139|\tfor offset, line := range subsection {\n140|\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n141|\t\tif trimmed == \"```text\" || trimmed == \"```\" {\n142|\t\t\tfences = append(fences, offset)\n143|\t\t}\n144|\t}\n145|\tif len(fences) != 2 || strings.TrimSpace(subsection[fences[0]]) != \"```text\" {\n146|\t\tt.Fatalf(\"README subsection at line %d carries %d fence lines, want exactly one ```text block\", start, len(fences))\n147|\t}\n148|\tgot := strings.Join(subsection[fences[0]+1:fences[1]], \"\\n\")\n149|\tif got != want {\n150|\t\tt.Fatalf(\"README fenced coverage line = %q, tracecheck printed %q\", got, want)\n151|\t}\n152|}\n153|\n154|// assertProseFigure requires the pattern to match the unwrapped subsection\n155|// exactly once and each capture to equal the measured figure. A capture that\n156|// is all digits compares numerically; any other capture decodes through the\n157|// closed number-word map.\n158|func assertProseFigure(t *testing.